00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
now next this afternoon boris johnson  has pulled out of a major conference  looking at the issues around leveling up  in northern england he had been expected  to address a meeting held by  conservative mps cabinet members local  councillors and also business leaders in  doncaster but our deputy political  editor sam coats is in doncaster for us  this afternoon waiting patiently uh any  reason do we know some why boris  johnson's not going to be there  we don't know yet we're expecting an  announcement from downing street in the  next hour or so we are assured that it's  a good reason for him pulling out uh  that uh of what could have been quite a  sticky conference here at the northern  research group's annual first annual  conference here at doncaster racecourse  the conference is going on  next to me hundreds of conservative mps  from the north activists business people  all gathered here to put pressure on the  party to try and give a greater focus uh  to the people of the north try and win  uh once again some of those red wool  seats potentially even uh to win uh some  new ones uh now that's been the job  today but boris johnson uh is a no show  the organizers were only told in the  last couple of hours uh they are  mystified they don't know the reason yet  uh but they are they are assured that is  of significant  sufficient significance that nobody will  ask any questions about whether or not  this event was just too sticky uh for  the prime minister uh to attend so it's  continuing to go on and i'm joined by a  couple of people that have turned up uh  chris green is the mp for bolton west  and katie burgess is a former mayoral  candidate for liverpool not known uh for  being a traditional conservative  heartland casey start with you  boris johnson has particular  significance in liverpool not  necessarily in a good way how is he  going down now and what does he need to  do to reassure  the voters in your area  i think in liverpool boris will always  be boris i don't think his reputation  will improve or deteriorate regardless  of what he does i think generally people  want to see him deliver on promises they  want to see this government improve the  area like they promised they did and  regardless of who they're voting for  locally they want westminster to keep  those promises to hold those people to  account and to deliver what we're  wanting do you think boris johnson is  the best person to lead the conservative  party as far as people in your area is  concerned uh probably not for merseyside  um he came with a reputation um that  said i think there are people who have  warned to him there are certainly  younger voters who like boris he offers  something that they maybe haven't seen  before or that they don't see in  politics  certain things that have gone on we've  had covert we've had things but it's i  think ultimately um he's not doing any  damage to the area i think if anything  else um chris green this is an event  where  frankly boris jones was probably going  to come here face northern mps thoughts  face some of those figures he didn't  vote for him in that vote of no  confidence uh in order to as it were uh  renew his marriage vows to the people  here  how do you think his no-show will go  down and just before you answer that did  you vote for him in the vote of my  confidence uh well it's a it's a private  vote so i'm not going to comment  publicly because i think it's unfair and  colleagues uh because the media is going  to play that game or kind of narrow down  who's on which side and that  factionalism i don't think that's very  useful it would have been great if the  prime minister had been able to make it  and i don't know why he's been uh called  away but i don't think it's a fear uh  factor i think this is a really positive  conference the northern research group  is setting out a pretty good agenda for  the north of england boris johnson is  enthusiastic about that so i think this  would have should have been a great  event for him and i don't think he's too  worried about the reception because i  know it would be very good you've just  heard some of the criticism of  people in the area about boris johnson  do you think that boris johnson is still  a vote winner in places like this uh  yeah i'm from liverpool as well so  all politicians of any background have  a mixed feeling uh locally wherever it  might be uh but i think boris johnson  has huge skills and yeah we we  appreciate the vote uh recently uh  amongst uh conservative members of  parliament that boris johnson is best  when he's under pressure he's under a  bit of pressure now and this is where he  i think he's going to come out and start  delivering  what do you think the people in the room  in there the voters in this area need to  hear from the conservatives what is the  policy suggestion or the direction of  travel that you think is missing needs  to be re-injected into the debate  i think with our leveling up agenda we  need to see that leveling up of  opportunity and i think it's about  gaining consensus for what people need  and want and it's a fine balance so we  don't help people when we do it for them  but if we can create an opportunity that  leads to people then taking those  opportunities and building for  themselves it's about independence um  chris i sometimes listen to debates  particularly amongst northern  conservatives about what direction you  should take leveling up and all i hear  is spending spending spending spending  on subsidies spending on investment  is that very conservative does that ever  concern you  it does concern me and not always very  conservative and i was quite pleased to  be on the the panel about net zero which  is quite an expensive area if you get  into the detail now i think there ought  to be a huge agenda on cutting the cost  of energy prices getting the reshoring  the return on manufacturing uh to  britain but also i think that will  disproportionately come to the north of  england so this is what i want to see  and i think the the positive agenda the  government's got is hinckley c seiswell  uh is going to have a nuclear power  station the small modular reactor agenda  this isn't about subsidies fundamentally  this is about making sure the united  kingdom has affordable energy to make  sure we can power our industry  you hear a lot of different things  including from cabinet ministers about  what even this government's economic  policy is some want to cut taxes some  want to reduce debt some want to target  inflation uh some want to deregulate  somewhat to help people out by uh making  the banks lend to riskier people does  this government even have an economic  policy i think there needs to be a more  clarity on this and one of the  challenges has been during covet uh we  had to act in a way which was a bit out  of kilter with traditional conservative  approaches we had to intervene more and  the repercussions of kovit and the  lockdown as well whether it's an  education whether it's in business  whether it's in the wider healthcare  means we have to intervene more than we  would like but we have to get through  the other side of this thank you very  much both indeed uh you had a point of  view maybe boris johnson not best for  the merseyside region at least and also  a conservative mp not quite sure that he  can say wholeheartedly this government  has an economic policy lots of  challenges that boris johnson wasn't  able to address today all right so many  thanks indeed sam coach sarah doncaster

